# Installed as /etc/journal-digest/ignore by install-journal-digest.sh, which overwrites edits made there.
#
# Failures and errors which journal-digest leaves out of its daily report. One Python regular expression per line,
# searched for in the line the report shows: "unit (system): result" or "unit (user): result" for a failed unit,
# "identifier: message" for an error, and "crash of executable (package): signal" for a crash report. Empty lines and
# lines starting with # are skipped.
#
# Try patterns out before installing them with: ./journal-digest.py --since -7d --ignore journal-digest.ignore

# Short-lived autostart commands which often exit before gnome-session asks systemd to move them into their scope,
# which systemd then refuses ("No PIDs left to attach"). The commands themselves ran.
^app-gnome-(ubuntu\\x2dreport\\x2don\\x2dupgrade|snap\\x2duserd\\x2dautostart)-[0-9]+\.scope \(user\): resources$
^systemd: Failed to start app-gnome-(ubuntu\\x2dreport\\x2don\\x2dupgrade|snap\\x2duserd\\x2dautostart)-[0-9]+\.scope

# Garbled replies from the Framework laptop's embedded controller, a few to a few dozen a day since late October, for
# which the kernel fails the command. What would matter, the fan not following the heat, is watched by the CPU
# Throttle Indicator extension. The embedded controller's trouble is reported upstream as
# FrameworkComputer/EmbeddedController issue 69.
^kernel: cros_ec_lpcs \S+: (bad packet checksum|packet too long)

# The embedded controller, which answers the kernel's USB-C questions (UCSI), rejects reading the power offers of a
# charger or dock as an incompatible partner (-95) when the kernel asks right after it is plugged in or the laptop
# resumes, while later reads list them. It also answers the question for the alternate modes of a cable plug with
# UCSI's undefined error, bit 8, for every occupied port at every boot and every time the dock is plugged in. Charging
# is negotiated by the embedded controller and works regardless. Both are described in
# FrameworkComputer/EmbeddedController issue 72. Rarer UCSI errors stay in the report.
^kernel: ucsi_acpi \S+: UCSI_GET_PDOS failed \(-95\)$
^kernel: ucsi_acpi \S+: unknown error 256$

# At every login gnome-keyring's PAM module looks for the keyring daemon before the session has started it, logs this
# as an error, keeps the password, and unlocks the keyring with it once the session is open ("unlocked login keyring"
# follows). Upstream explained it with the "stashed password" line in 2020 instead of changing the level.
^gdm-[a-z]+\]: gkr-pam: unable to locate daemon control file$

# With Bluetooth turned off (blocked through rfkill), bluetoothd tries to power the adapter on at every start anyway,
# and the kernel answers with a generic failure. Fixed upstream by bluez commit aa3d6c761c ("adapter: Don't power on
# while blocked by rfkill"), after 5.87, and by kernel commit 7eac806143 ("Bluetooth: MGMT: Return RFKILLED for Set
# Powered on a blocked controller"). Remove this once Ubuntu ships a bluez which includes the first.
^bluetoothd: Failed to set mode: (Failed \(0x03\)|Blocked through rfkill \(0x12\))$

# The WD_BLACK SN8100 NVMe drive advertises a Data Object Exchange mailbox, but never answers the kernel's discovery
# request on it, so at every boot the kernel gives the mailbox up with these two errors. DOE serves device
# authentication and CXL, and the drive works as usual. The same drive is traced in the linux-pci patch "PCI/DOE: Add
# debug messages for transaction failures", where a quirk to skip it is asked about:
# https://lore.kernel.org/all/20260721193310.1179300-1-saverio.pub2@gmail.com/
^kernel: pci \S+: DOE: \[500\] failed to (cache features : -5|create mailbox: -5)$
